    <section class="dscr-why-section" id="why-dscr">
      <div class="section-label">WHY INVESTORS CHOOSE DSCR</div>
      <h2>Why use a DSCR loan instead of conventional financing?</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">DSCR financing can be useful when the property's rental economics and an investor's financing strategy fit a program better than a conventional agency loan. It is not automatically cheaper or better; compare the complete terms and trade-offs.</p>
      <div class="benefit-grid">
        <article><span>01</span><h3>Personal income may be less central</h3><p>Many DSCR programs qualify primarily around the property's eligible rental income and debt service rather than requiring the same personal income and DTI documentation used in conventional underwriting. Credit, assets, liquidity, and guarantor reviews may still apply.</p></article>
        <article><span>02</span><h3>Self-employed and complex tax returns</h3><p>Investors with business income, depreciation, write-offs, multiple entities, or complex returns may prefer a program that does not rely on traditional personal-income qualification in the same way.</p></article>
        <article><span>03</span><h3>Portfolio growth beyond agency limits</h3><p>Some DSCR lenders permit more than 10 financed properties or use different portfolio-exposure tests. This is lender-specific—not a universal DSCR feature—and the lender may still cap total loans, balances, or guarantor exposure.</p></article>
        <article><span>04</span><h3>Entity ownership may be available</h3><p>Some DSCR programs allow eligible LLC or other business-entity borrowers or vesting structures. Ownership, personal guaranties, operating documents, and state-specific requirements vary by lender.</p></article>
        <article><span>05</span><h3>Underwriting tied to rental performance</h3><p>The rent-to-PITIA relationship can make the property's income capacity a central part of the review. Lenders may use leases, appraiser rent schedules, market-rent analysis, or program-specific short-term-rental methods.</p></article>
        <article><span>06</span><h3>Portfolio and cash-flow flexibility</h3><p>Depending on the lender, DSCR options may support purchases, rate-term refinances, cash-out refinances, different property types, or repeat investor transactions with program-specific documentation.</p></article>
      </div>
      <div class="agency-note"><b>Trade-offs to compare before choosing DSCR:</b> Rates and fees may be higher than conventional financing; down payments and reserve requirements may be substantial; minimum DSCR, credit score, property condition, appraisal, loan-size, prepayment-penalty, and guaranty rules can apply. Some lenders require personal guarantees even when an LLC is the borrower. Compare APR, points, closing costs, prepayment terms, recourse/guaranty obligations, cash-to-close, and total financing cost—not just the qualifying method.</div>
      <p class="fine-print">A DSCR loan is not a promise of approval and does not remove the need to document ownership, funds, credit, property eligibility, or other lender requirements. Program terms vary by lender and state.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="agency-explainer-section" id="who-are-the-agencies">
      <div class="section-label">WHO'S WHO IN THE MORTGAGE MARKET</div>
      <h2>What are Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac—and what do they actually do?</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">You may hear mortgage professionals refer to “the agencies,” “conventional agency loans,” or “conforming loans.” These names describe a part of the U.S. housing-finance system—not the bank or mortgage broker taking your application.</p>
      <div class="agency-definition-grid">
        <article>
          <div class="agency-acronym">FNMA</div>
          <h3><a href="https://www.fanniemae.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae ↗</a></h3>
          <p><b>Federal National Mortgage Association.</b> Created by Congress in 1938, Fannie Mae is a government-sponsored enterprise (GSE). It does not generally make home loans directly to consumers. It buys eligible mortgages from approved lenders, pools them into mortgage-backed securities (MBS), and guarantees investors timely payment of principal and interest on those securities, subject to its guarantee terms.</p>
          <p>That secondary-market activity helps lenders replenish funds so they can make additional mortgages. Fannie Mae also publishes underwriting and servicing requirements for loans it will purchase or otherwise support.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <div class="agency-acronym">FHLMC</div>
          <h3><a href="https://www.freddiemac.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Freddie Mac ↗</a></h3>
          <p><b>Federal Home Loan Mortgage Corporation.</b> Created by Congress in 1970, Freddie Mac is also a government-sponsored enterprise. Like Fannie Mae, it generally works in the secondary mortgage market rather than making ordinary consumer mortgages directly.</p>
          <p>It purchases eligible mortgages from approved lenders, securitizes them, and provides guarantees on its mortgage-backed securities. Its purchase and underwriting requirements help shape the conventional mortgage products lenders offer.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="agency-flow">
        <div><b>1 · Borrower</b><span>Applies with a bank, credit union, or mortgage company</span></div>
        <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
        <div><b>2 · Lender</b><span>Underwrites, closes, and funds the mortgage</span></div>
        <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
        <div><b>3 · Secondary market</b><span>Eligible loan may be sold to Fannie Mae or Freddie Mac</span></div>
        <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
        <div><b>4 · Mortgage-backed security</b><span>Loans are pooled; agency guarantee supports investor payments</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="agency-note"><b>What this means for a borrower:</b> Your lender or mortgage broker is the company you work with to apply and close. Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac usually do not approve your application across a desk or service your loan themselves. But if your lender plans to sell the loan to one of them, the agency's eligibility, documentation, appraisal, credit, debt, reserve, and property rules influence how that loan is underwritten.</div>
      <div class="agency-compare-grid">
        <article><h3>What is a conforming loan?</h3><p>A conventional mortgage that meets the applicable requirements for purchase or securitization by Fannie Mae or Freddie Mac, including loan limits and underwriting standards. “Conventional” means it is not insured or guaranteed by FHA, VA, or USDA; it does not automatically mean the loan conforms to agency requirements.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Who oversees them?</h3><p>The Federal Housing Finance Agency (FHFA) is the federal regulator and conservator of Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac. FHFA supervises their safety and soundness and their conservatorships. The GSEs are not ordinary federal departments, and their securities are not the same as U.S. Treasury securities.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Where does Ginnie Mae fit?</h3><p>Ginnie Mae (Government National Mortgage Association) is a wholly government-owned corporation within HUD. It guarantees timely payment on mortgage-backed securities backed by federally insured or guaranteed loans, such as FHA, VA, and USDA loans. It does not operate like Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac by buying conventional mortgages for its own portfolio.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="fine-print">A GSE guarantee on an MBS is not a guarantee that an individual borrower will repay the mortgage, and it does not mean the federal government guarantees every conventional mortgage. Program rules, guarantee terms, and investor risk differ. This is a simplified educational overview.</p>
      <div class="resource-links">
        <a href="https://www.fhfa.gov/about-fhfa" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FHFA: About the regulator ↗</a>
        <a href="https://www.fanniemae.com/about-us" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae: About us ↗</a>
        <a href="https://www.freddiemac.com/about" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Freddie Mac: About us ↗</a>
        <a href="https://www.ginniemae.gov/about_us/Pages/our_mission.aspx" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ginnie Mae: Mission and role ↗</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mortgage-standards-section" id="non-qm-subprime-history">
      <div class="section-label">THE EVOLUTION OF MORTGAGE LENDING</div>
      <h2>From subprime to Non-QM: what changed after the financial crisis?</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">“Non-QM” is a modern mortgage category. It is not simply a new name for the subprime loans that helped fuel the 2000s housing crisis. Understanding the difference starts with the history—and with the rules Congress and regulators put in place afterward.</p>
      <div class="mortgage-standards-callout">
        <b>Two labels that describe different things:</b>
        <p><b>Subprime</b> traditionally describes a borrower's credit-risk tier. <b>QM / Non-QM</b> describes whether a covered consumer mortgage meets a legal Qualified Mortgage definition under federal rules. They are not opposite ends of one scale. A borrower with strong credit but complex or difficult-to-document income may need a Non-QM program; a borrower with weaker credit may still qualify for a QM loan if the loan meets the applicable requirements. The actual lender's underwriting and the law—not the label—matter.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="mortgage-history-timeline">
        <article><span>1990s–mid-2000s</span><h3>Subprime and Alt-A expand</h3><p>Mortgage credit expanded to borrowers who did not meet prime-market standards. Alt-A generally described a broad middle category between prime and subprime; borrowers might have stronger credit but loans could involve limited documentation or other elevated-risk features. Some lenders also offered stated-income or low-documentation loans, high-risk adjustable-rate structures, interest-only payments, layered risk features, and loans with weak verification. Not every subprime loan was abusive, and not every borrower was unable to repay; the problem was the scale of weak underwriting, poor product design, misaligned incentives, and loans made without a sound assessment of repayment ability.</p></article>
        <article><span>2006–2009</span><h3>Housing downturn and foreclosure crisis</h3><p>As home prices stopped rising and then fell in many markets, borrowers could no longer rely on refinancing or selling to escape unaffordable payments. Defaults and foreclosures spread through mortgage-backed securities and financial institutions, contributing to the 2007–2009 financial crisis and severe economic damage.</p></article>
        <article><span>July 21, 2010</span><h3>Dodd-Frank becomes law</h3><p>The Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act responded to broad weaknesses exposed by the crisis. Its mortgage provisions amended the Truth in Lending Act (TILA), created the Ability-to-Repay framework, established Qualified Mortgage protections, restricted certain loan features and compensation practices, and expanded consumer-protection oversight.</p></article>
        <article><span>January 2013 onward</span><h3>ATR/QM rules take shape</h3><p>The CFPB issued the implementing Ability-to-Repay / Qualified Mortgage rule in January 2013 under Regulation Z. Later amendments changed the QM definition and implementation details. The current rules—not the original 2013 summary alone—must be consulted for a particular loan.</p></article>
        <article><span>Today</span><h3>Prime, government, Non-QM and business-purpose options</h3><p>The market includes agency-conforming loans, FHA/VA/USDA programs, consumer-purpose Non-QM loans, and business-purpose investor financing such as many DSCR programs. Each category has its own legal scope, underwriting standards, investor requirements, and lender overlays.</p></article>
      </div>

      <div class="mortgage-standards-grid">
        <article>
          <div class="standards-tag">THE RULE</div>
          <h3>Ability-to-Repay (ATR)</h3>
          <p>For a covered consumer-purpose residential mortgage, the creditor generally must make a reasonable, good-faith determination before closing that the consumer can repay according to the loan terms. The creditor must consider and verify the applicable factors under Regulation Z, rather than relying only on the home's value or the hope that prices will rise.</p>
          <p>The eight general factors include current or reasonably expected income or assets (not the collateral home's value), employment status when employment income is used, the proposed mortgage payment, simultaneous loans, mortgage-related obligations, current debts and support obligations, debt-to-income ratio or residual income, and credit history. Applicable rules require verification of information the creditor relies upon.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <div class="standards-tag">THE CATEGORY</div>
          <h3>Qualified Mortgage (QM)</h3>
          <p>A QM is a covered consumer mortgage that meets a regulatory definition and its applicable product-feature, points-and-fees, and underwriting requirements. QM status can provide a creditor or assignee with a safe harbor or a rebuttable presumption of compliance with ATR, depending on the type and pricing of the loan.</p>
          <p>QM is not a government guarantee, and it does not mean every borrower is guaranteed to repay. It is a defined regulatory category with specific legal consequences.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <div class="standards-tag">THE OTHER CATEGORY</div>
          <h3>Non-QM</h3>
          <p>Non-QM generally means a covered consumer mortgage that does not meet a Qualified Mortgage definition. It may use alternative documentation or underwriting methods—such as bank statements, asset-based analysis, or other permitted methods—depending on the program.</p>
          <p><b>Non-QM does not automatically mean “no-doc,” “no verification,” or “no ATR.”</b> A consumer-purpose loan that is not QM generally remains subject to applicable ATR requirements unless a specific statutory or regulatory exemption applies. The lender must have a lawful repayment analysis and follow other applicable federal and state requirements.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="mortgage-standards-callout">
        <b>Important distinction: Non-QM is a QM classification—not a blanket exemption from mortgage law.</b>
        <p>A lender cannot simply label a consumer mortgage “Non-QM” and ignore ATR. The legal question is first whether the transaction is covered by the consumer-credit rules, then which ATR/QM provisions and any applicable exemptions apply. Product names and marketing labels do not decide that question.</p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="standards-subhead">Why Non-QM is different from pre-crisis subprime</h3>
      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="mortgage-comparison">
          <thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Pre-crisis weak subprime practices</th><th>Modern responsible Non-QM approach</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th>Repayment analysis</th><td>Some loans were originated with little meaningful assessment of whether the borrower could afford the payments.</td><td>Covered consumer loans remain subject to ATR unless exempt; underwriting should document a supportable repayment method.</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Income documentation</th><td>Stated-income and low-documentation products were sometimes used without reliable verification.</td><td>Alternative documentation can be used where permitted, but the lender must follow the applicable legal standard and program rules.</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Loan structure</th><td>Some products combined teaser rates, payment shock, negative amortization, or other risky features.</td><td>Non-QM programs vary; borrowers should understand the fully indexed or future payment, amortization, balloon features, and prepayment terms.</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Collateral and equity</th><td>In some cases, lenders relied heavily on rising property values and the ability to refinance.</td><td>Property value and leverage matter, but they should not substitute for required repayment analysis on a covered consumer loan.</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Investor and lender controls</th><td>Origination, securitization, ratings, and risk controls failed in multiple parts of the market.</td><td>Modern programs have lender, investor, compliance, disclosure, licensing, and state-law requirements that vary by product and transaction.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="fine-print">This is a comparison of broad market patterns, not a claim that every pre-crisis subprime loan was defective or every current Non-QM loan is sound. Non-QM quality depends on actual underwriting, product terms, borrower circumstances, and applicable law.</p>

      <h3 class="standards-subhead">Where DSCR fits—and why many DSCR loans are treated differently</h3>
      <p>Many DSCR loans are made to finance non-owner-occupied rental property for an investment or business purpose. Regulation Z generally excludes credit extended primarily for a business, commercial, agricultural, or organizational purpose. The official commentary specifically treats credit to acquire, improve, or maintain non-owner-occupied rental property as business-purpose credit, regardless of the number of units, subject to the stated conditions and facts of the transaction.</p>
      <p>That is a different legal path from a consumer-purpose mortgage on a borrower's principal dwelling. For a qualifying business-purpose rental loan, the consumer ATR/QM framework in Regulation Z generally does not apply in the same way. This is <b>not</b> because “DSCR” is a magic exemption or because all Non-QM loans are exempt; it is because the transaction's genuine primary purpose may be business-purpose credit under the regulation.</p>
      <div class="dscr-law-grid">
        <article><h3>Typical non-owner-occupied DSCR scenario</h3><p>An investor obtains financing to acquire or refinance a rental property that will be held for investment and rented to others. The lender may focus on property rent, PITIA, credit, liquidity, reserves, leverage, entity structure, and guarantor strength. The transaction is commonly treated as business-purpose credit when the regulatory criteria are met.</p></article>
        <article><h3>When the analysis changes</h3><p>If the borrower will occupy the property, use it as a personal residence, or the facts point to a consumer purpose, do not assume the business-purpose exclusion applies. Owner-occupied rental properties have different rules and specific unit-count tests. Mixed-purpose, personal-use, and unusual ownership structures require careful review.</p></article>
      </div>
      <div class="mortgage-standards-callout">
        <b>DSCR is an underwriting method; business purpose is a legal-purpose determination.</b>
        <p>A loan can be marketed as DSCR or Non-QM, but the label alone does not determine whether it is consumer or business credit. Lenders and brokers must evaluate the actual purpose, occupancy, borrower, property use, and governing rules. State law, licensing, disclosure, anti-fraud, fair-lending, and other requirements may still apply even when a federal Regulation Z business-purpose exclusion is available.</p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="standards-subhead">Dodd-Frank and the mortgage rules borrowers may hear about</h3>
      <div class="dodd-frank-grid">
        <article><b>ATR / QM</b><p>Created the statutory framework requiring a reasonable ability-to-repay determination for covered consumer mortgages and establishing QM categories with specified legal protections.</p></article>
        <article><b>Loan originator standards</b><p>Addressed compensation and steering practices, including restrictions designed to reduce incentives to steer consumers into loans based on terms that benefit the originator rather than the borrower.</p></article>
        <article><b>High-cost mortgage protections</b><p>Expanded and amended protections for certain high-cost mortgages, building on the Home Ownership and Equity Protection Act (HOEPA).</p></article>
        <article><b>Prepayment penalties</b><p>Restricted prepayment penalties for covered mortgage transactions and placed limits on when they may be permitted.</p></article>
        <article><b>Mortgage servicing and disclosures</b><p>Added or amended requirements affecting servicing, disclosures, and consumer protections across the mortgage lifecycle.</p></article>
        <article><b>CFPB oversight</b><p>Created the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau and consolidated important federal consumer-finance rulemaking and enforcement responsibilities, including TILA and RESPA functions.</p></article>
      </div>

      <div class="resource-links">
        <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/rules-policy/final-rules/ability-to-repay-qualified-mortgage-rule/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CFPB: ATR/QM rule and amendments ↗</a>
        <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/rules-policy/regulations/1026/43/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Regulation Z §1026.43: ATR and QM ↗</a>
        <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/rules-policy/regulations/1026/3/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Regulation Z §1026.3: Business-purpose exemptions ↗</a>
        <a href="https://www.federalreservehistory.org/essays/dodd-frank-act" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Federal Reserve History: Dodd-Frank Act ↗</a>
      </div>
      <p class="fine-print">Educational overview only, not legal or compliance advice. The application of TILA, Regulation Z, Dodd-Frank provisions, business-purpose exclusions, and state law depends on the facts of each transaction. Consult qualified compliance counsel for a specific loan scenario.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="property-count-section">
      <div class="section-label">CONVENTIONAL AGENCY GUIDELINES</div>
      <h2>Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac: the 10-property rule.</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">Fannie Mae's DU policy generally caps second-home and investment-property transactions at 10 financed properties. Freddie Mac's investment-property mortgage policy generally caps borrowers at 10 financed 1–4-unit properties. Both rules include the subject property and the borrower's financed primary residence. These are agency underwriting limits—not legal limits on how many properties someone may own.</p>
      <div class="count-layout">
        <div class="count-highlight"><strong>10</strong><span>Generally the maximum number of financed 1–4-unit residential properties for eligible Fannie Mae DU second-home/investment transactions and Freddie Mac investment-property transactions.</span></div>
        <div>
          <p><b>Fannie Mae:</b> Its Selling Guide B2-2-03 specifies a DU maximum of 10 financed properties for second-home and investment-property transactions. The policy also includes reserve requirements that increase with the number of other financed properties.</p>
          <p><b>Freddie Mac:</b> Seller/Servicer Guide 4201.13 generally limits each borrower individually and all borrowers collectively to no more than 10 financed 1–4-unit properties, including the subject and primary residence, for investment-property mortgages.</p>
          <p><b>How properties are counted matters.</b> The rules generally focus on properties for which the borrower is personally obligated on the debt. Commercial real estate, 5+ unit multifamily, vacant land, and certain other categories are excluded. Both agencies describe exclusions for property titled in a borrower's business when the borrower is not individually obligated on its debt. A co-borrower's properties and jointly financed properties can affect the calculation.</p>
          <p><b>An LLC is not an automatic workaround.</b> The title, note, guaranty, and actual debt obligations matter. Do not assume a property disappears from the count merely because an LLC is on title.</p>
          <div class="resource-links">
            <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b2-2-03/multiple-financed-properties-same-borrower" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae Selling Guide B2-2-03 ↗</a>
            <a href="https://guide.freddiemac.com/ci/okcsFattach/get/1002095_2" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Freddie Mac Seller/Servicer Guide 4201.13 (PDF) ↗</a>
            <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b3-4.1-01/minimum-reserve-requirements" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae reserve requirements ↗</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="agency-note"><b>Where DSCR financing differs:</b> DSCR/non-QM programs are not governed by the Fannie Mae or Freddie Mac 10-property rule simply because they are mortgages. Each DSCR lender sets its own limits for properties owned, financed properties, aggregate exposure, loan count, borrower/guarantor requirements, reserves, and eligible entity vesting. Some programs may allow investors to go beyond 10 financed properties; others may impose a lower cap. Verify the specific lender's current matrix before relying on it.</div>
      <p class="fine-print">Guidelines can change and exceptions are program-specific. This is a high-level educational summary, not a loan eligibility determination. The current agency Selling/Seller-Servicer Guides control.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="facts-section">
      <div class="section-label">INVESTOR QUICK FACTS</div>
      <h2>A few useful things to know before you make an offer.</h2>
      <div class="facts-grid">
        <article><b>1.00×</b><h3>Break-even coverage example</h3><p>A 1.00× ratio means qualifying rent equals the PITIA amount used in the calculation.</p></article>
        <article><b>1.25×</b><h3>More rent than PITIA</h3><p>A 1.25× ratio means $1.25 in qualifying monthly rent for every $1.00 of monthly PITIA.</p></article>
        <article><b>Rent matters</b><h3>Documentation can vary</h3><p>Lenders may use an existing lease, appraiser rent schedule, or other program-approved rental evidence.</p></article>
        <article><b>Not net profit</b><h3>DSCR is not cash in your pocket</h3><p>A simple rent-to-PITIA ratio does not subtract every operating cost, vacancy, repair, or capital expense.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

