    <section class="rate-education-section" id="how-mortgage-rates-work">
      <div class="section-label">UNDERSTANDING THE RATE</div>
      <h2>The Fed does not set your 30-year mortgage rate.</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">The Federal Reserve influences the cost of money throughout the economy, but a 30-year fixed mortgage is priced in the bond and mortgage-backed-securities markets. That is why mortgage rates can move before a Fed meeting, move in the opposite direction after an announcement, or barely move at all.</p>
      <div class="rate-mechanics-grid">
        <article><span>01 · THE FED</span><h3>Federal funds rate</h3><p>The FOMC sets a target range for the overnight rate banks use when lending reserve balances to one another. It strongly influences short-term borrowing costs, adjustable-rate products, and broader financial conditions. It is not the rate on a 30-year fixed mortgage.</p></article>
        <article><span>02 · THE BOND MARKET</span><h3>Treasury yields & expectations</h3><p>Investors price longer-term bonds based on expected inflation, future short-term rates, economic growth, government borrowing, and demand for safe assets. The 10-year Treasury yield is a widely watched mortgage-market reference—not a direct formula.</p></article>
        <article><span>03 · MORTGAGE BONDS</span><h3>Mortgage-backed securities</h3><p>Mortgage rates are closely connected to the yields investors demand on mortgage-backed securities (MBS). MBS pricing also reflects prepayment risk: when rates fall, borrowers may refinance sooner, changing the expected life and return of the security.</p></article>
        <article><span>04 · THE LOAN OFFER</span><h3>Spread, risk & lender pricing</h3><p>The final rate also reflects the spread between MBS yields and Treasury yields, market volatility, lender capacity and margins, loan size, occupancy, credit, LTV, points, and product features. Two borrowers can receive different quotes on the same day.</p></article>
      </div>
      <div class="rate-flow" aria-label="How a mortgage rate is formed">
        <div><b>Fed policy & economic outlook</b><small>Influence short-term rates and expectations</small></div><i>→</i>
        <div><b>Treasury yields & MBS prices</b><small>Reflect investor demand, inflation, risk and prepayment</small></div><i>→</i>
        <div><b>Lender pricing</b><small>Adds market spreads, costs and borrower/product adjustments</small></div>
      </div>
      <div class="rate-callout">
        <b>Why a Fed rate cut does not guarantee a mortgage-rate drop</b>
        <p>Bond investors are pricing what they expect to happen next. If a Fed cut was already expected, it may already be reflected in Treasury and MBS prices. If investors fear persistent inflation or rising federal borrowing, longer-term yields can rise even while the Fed cuts its overnight target.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rate-mechanics-grid policy-grid">
        <article><span>WHEN THE ECONOMY WEAKENS</span><h3>Rates may fall to support activity</h3><p>When demand and employment weaken or inflation is too low, the Fed may lower its policy rate and use other tools to make financial conditions easier. Lower borrowing costs can encourage households and businesses to spend, invest, and hire. Low rates can be a response to trouble—not proof that the economy is thriving.</p></article>
        <article><span>WHEN INFLATION RUNS HOT</span><h3>Rates may rise to cool demand</h3><p>When inflation is too high, the Fed may raise short-term rates to restrain borrowing and spending. Bond yields can also rise because investors demand compensation for expected inflation and uncertainty. The goal is price stability, not simply the lowest possible mortgage payment.</p></article>
      </div>
      <div class="rate-callout">
        <b>Important distinction</b>
        <p>Low rates are not automatically a sign of an unhealthy economy. They can reflect a healthy low-inflation environment, strong demand for bonds, or a weak economy that needs support. The context matters. The 2020–2021 mortgage-rate lows occurred during extraordinary pandemic-era monetary support and market conditions; they should not be treated as the normal baseline.</p>
      </div>

