    <section class="faq-section">
      <div class="section-label">DSCR LOAN FAQ</div>
      <h2>Common questions from rental property investors</h2>
      <details><summary>Do I need to provide personal tax returns for a DSCR loan?</summary><p>Many DSCR programs focus on the subject property's qualifying rental income rather than using personal tax-return income as the primary qualification method. A lender may still request other personal, business, asset, credit, or entity documentation.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Does a DSCR loan require a 1.00 ratio?</summary><p>Not every program has the same minimum. Some require a ratio at or above 1.00×, while others may consider lower ratios with different pricing, leverage, credit, or reserve requirements. Program rules change, so the specific guideline must be checked.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I close a DSCR loan in an LLC?</summary><p>Some DSCR programs permit eligible entities such as LLCs, often with personal guarantor requirements. Entity eligibility, ownership structure, and documentation vary by lender and state.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I use short-term rental income?</summary><p>Some programs allow short-term rental income when the property type, market, and rental documentation meet program requirements. Other programs restrict or exclude it.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I refinance a rental property and take cash out?</summary><p>Some DSCR programs offer cash-out refinancing for eligible investment properties. Maximum leverage, seasoning, loan size, credit, reserves, and use-of-proceeds rules vary.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Does a higher DSCR guarantee loan approval?</summary><p>No. DSCR is only one part of underwriting. The lender also reviews the borrower or guarantor, property, loan structure, documentation, and applicable program requirements.</p></details>
    </section>


    <section class="llc-section" id="llc-basics">
      <div class="section-label">OWNERSHIP & ENTITY BASICS</div>
      <h2>Should you hold a rental property in an LLC?</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">An LLC can create a separate legal entity for an investment activity and may help separate business operations from personal affairs when it is properly formed and maintained. An LLC does not automatically create tax savings: federal tax treatment depends on the number of owners and any elections made. It is not automatic asset protection or a substitute for appropriate insurance and legal advice.</p>
      <div class="llc-grid">
        <article><h3>Potential advantages</h3><ul><li>Separates business records, contracts, and banking from personal activity</li><li>May help limit an owner's personal liability for certain entity obligations, subject to exceptions and proper operation</li><li>Can provide a structure for multiple owners, succession, and operating agreements</li><li>May make property-level accounting and partner reporting easier</li><li>May offer tax-classification flexibility, depending on ownership and elections</li></ul></article>
        <article><h3>Important trade-offs</h3><ul><li>Formation, annual filings, registered-agent, tax-preparation, and legal costs</li><li>Separate bookkeeping, banking, contracts, and records are essential</li><li>Personal guarantees can still create personal obligations</li><li>Insurance remains important; an LLC does not prevent lawsuits</li><li>Moving an already-financed property into an LLC can raise due-on-sale, title, insurance, tax, and lender-consent issues</li></ul></article>
      </div>
      <div class="llc-steps">
        <h3>Georgia LLC formation: a practical starting checklist</h3>
        <ol>
          <li><b>Choose the ownership plan.</b> Decide who owns the entity, who manages it, and whether one entity or separate property-level entities make sense. Discuss liability, financing, tax, and estate goals with qualified professionals.</li>
          <li><b>Choose a name and Georgia registered agent.</b> The registered agent must have a Georgia street address and be available for official service.</li>
          <li><b>File Articles of Organization.</b> Georgia's online domestic LLC filing currently lists a $110 total filing/service fee. The state advises that online processing time varies.</li>
          <li><b>Create an operating agreement.</b> It is an internal document that should address ownership, contributions, authority, distributions, transfers, and what happens if an owner exits. Have it tailored to the owners and property plan.</li>
          <li><b>Apply for an EIN directly with the IRS.</b> The IRS issues EINs for free. Form the legal entity first; avoid third-party sites that charge just to obtain an EIN.</li>
          <li><b>Open dedicated banking and bookkeeping.</b> Keep rents, deposits, expenses, and owner contributions traceable. Do not mix personal and rental funds.</li>
          <li><b>Check local licensing, zoning, tax, insurance, and lender requirements.</b> These depend on property location, use, ownership, and financing.</li>
          <li><b>Maintain the entity.</b> Georgia LLC annual registration is currently $60 and is due January 1–April 1 each year. Keep the registered agent and business details current.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="fine-print">Georgia fee and deadline snapshot; confirm current requirements before filing. Other states have different fees and rules. A lender may require personal guarantees, specific entity documents, or approval before title is transferred.</p>
        <div class="resource-links">
          <a href="https://ecorp.sos.ga.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Georgia Secretary of State filing portal ↗</a>
          <a href="https://sos.ga.gov/how-to-guide/how-guide-register-domestic-entity" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Georgia LLC formation instructions ↗</a>
