    <section class="management-section">
      <div class="section-label">PROPERTY MANAGEMENT</div>
      <h2>Self-manage or hire a property manager?</h2>
      <div class="management-grid">
        <div><h3>Questions to ask a manager</h3><ul><li>What is the full fee schedule, including leasing, renewal, maintenance coordination, and vacancy fees?</li><li>Who holds security deposits and how are owner funds reported?</li><li>How are vendors selected, insured, and approved?</li><li>What is the emergency response process and after-hours coverage?</li><li>How often are inspections performed and how are photos shared?</li><li>What is the termination process and who owns the tenant/listing records?</li></ul></div>
        <div><h3>Common problems to plan for</h3><ul><li>Late rent or vacancy: written procedures, reserves, and lawful notices</li><li>Water intrusion or mold concerns: prompt response, documentation, and qualified remediation</li><li>HVAC/plumbing failure: vetted emergency vendors and clear spending limits</li><li>Guest complaints or damage: documented check-in/out inspections and response standards</li><li>Unexpected costs: reserve policy and approval thresholds</li><li>Weak bookkeeping: monthly reconciliation and property-level reporting</li></ul></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section rental-faq">
      <div class="section-label">RENTAL OWNER FAQ</div>
      <h2>Common questions and avoidable mistakes</h2>
      <details><summary>Does an LLC automatically protect my personal assets?</summary><p>No. Protection depends on state law, the facts of a claim, proper entity operation, adequate insurance, and other factors. Personal guarantees, personal wrongdoing, commingling, and certain statutory obligations can create personal exposure. Get legal advice for your circumstances.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I transfer a property with an existing mortgage into my LLC?</summary><p>Do not assume you can. Review the loan documents and obtain advice from the lender, title professional, and attorney before changing ownership. A transfer can affect due-on-sale provisions, title coverage, insurance, taxes, and future refinancing.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I use Airbnb income to qualify for a DSCR loan?</summary><p>Some investor programs accept short-term rental income using approved market-rent reports, historical booking records, or other documentation. Others restrict it or use different calculations. Confirm the exact program before relying on projected nightly income.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Do I need a permit to operate a short-term rental?</summary><p>Possibly. City and county zoning, registration, business licensing, occupancy limits, HOA rules, and tax requirements can apply. Check the exact property jurisdiction before purchasing or advertising.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Is Airbnb's tax collection the end of my tax responsibility?</summary><p>Not necessarily. Platform collection varies by jurisdiction and tax type. Confirm state, county, and city requirements and keep records of what the platform collected versus what remains your responsibility.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What is the difference between a repair and a capital improvement?</summary><p>The tax treatment can differ. Keep invoices and descriptions, and ask your tax professional to classify significant work. Do not automatically treat every renovation or replacement as a current-year repair deduction.</p></details>
      <details><summary>How much should I reserve for maintenance and vacancy?</summary><p>There is no universal percentage. It depends on property age, condition, systems, local vacancy, insurance deductibles, climate, and rental model. Use property-specific estimates and stress-test higher costs and lower occupancy.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What should I do when a tenant reports a serious repair?</summary><p>Respond promptly, follow the lease and applicable law, document the report and work, and use qualified contractors for safety-critical work. Georgia's landlord-tenant handbook explains repair and habitability responsibilities.</p></details>
    </section>

    <section class="housing-history-section" id="housing-history">
      <div class="section-label">THE LONG VIEW · U.S. HOUSING HISTORY</div>
      <h2>Mortgages, interest rates & home values through the decades</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">Put today's market in historical context. Explore how U.S. home values and 30-year mortgage rates have moved through different economic cycles. Long-term national trends are useful context—not a promise about any property's future value.</p>
      <div class="history-stat-grid">
        <article><strong>7 of 84</strong><span>Years with national home-price declines in the Shiller series, 1942–2025</span></article>
        <article><strong>16.64%</strong><span>Highest annual average 30-year fixed mortgage rate, 1981</span></article>
        <article><strong>2.96%</strong><span>Lowest annual average 30-year fixed mortgage rate, 2021</span></article>
      </div>

      <div class="economic-timeline" aria-label="Timeline of major U.S. economic events and their housing and mortgage effects">
        <div class="timeline-heading"><span class="history-kicker">THE ECONOMY BEHIND THE RATE</span><h3>What was happening in America?</h3><p>Mortgage rates do not move in a vacuum. Inflation, Federal Reserve policy, banking stress, and credit conditions all shape the cost and availability of home loans.</p></div>
        <ol class="timeline-list">
          <li><span class="timeline-date">1929–1933</span><div><h4>The crash and the Great Depression</h4><p>The stock-market crash was followed by waves of bank failures, collapsing credit, deflation, and unemployment. The bigger mortgage story was a breakdown in banking and housing finance—not simply a modern-style rate spike. Federal reforms later reshaped deposit protection and mortgage institutions.</p><small>Housing connection: credit became scarce, foreclosures surged, and long-term fixed-rate mortgage structures were still developing.</small></div></li>
          <li><span class="timeline-date">1934–1938</span><div><h4>New Deal housing reforms</h4><p>The FHA was created in 1934 to insure qualifying mortgages; Fannie Mae followed in 1938 to help provide a secondary market for FHA-insured loans.</p><small>Housing connection: federal mortgage insurance and a more organized mortgage funding system helped establish the modern U.S. home-finance market.</small></div></li>
          <li><span class="timeline-date">1940s–1960s</span><div><h4>World War II, postwar building, and the suburban boom</h4><p>Wartime finance and postwar policy kept borrowing costs comparatively constrained for a time. Returning veterans, highway construction, and large-scale homebuilding helped fuel suburban growth.</p><small>Housing connection: mortgage access expanded, and long-term amortizing home loans became central to household homeownership.</small></div></li>
          <li><span class="timeline-date">1971–1979</span><div><h4>Oil shocks and the Great Inflation</h4><p>The U.S. left dollar-gold convertibility in 1971. Oil disruptions in 1973–74 and 1978–79 pushed energy and production costs higher, while inflation expectations became entrenched.</p><small>Rate connection: lenders demanded protection against inflation, and the Fed faced growing pressure to tighten monetary policy.</small></div></li>
          <li><span class="timeline-date">1979–1982</span><div><h4>Volcker's inflation fight—and punishing mortgage rates</h4><p>Fed Chair Paul Volcker made defeating inflation the priority. The federal funds rate reached roughly 19% in 1981, and the 30-year mortgage rate annual average peaked at 16.64% that year.</p><small>Rate connection: borrowing became extraordinarily expensive; tight policy helped bring inflation down but contributed to a deep recession.</small></div></li>
          <li><span class="timeline-date">1980–1995</span><div><h4>The savings-and-loan crisis</h4><p>Many savings institutions had funded long-term, fixed-rate mortgages with deposits that repriced much faster. When rates surged, their funding costs rose while the value and income from older mortgages lagged. Deregulation, risky lending, and delayed resolutions worsened the crisis.</p><small>Housing connection: hundreds of thrifts ultimately failed, and the clean-up reshaped oversight of mortgage lenders.</small></div></li>
          <li><span class="timeline-date">1987</span><div><h4>Black Monday</h4><p>On October 19, stock markets plunged worldwide. The Federal Reserve supplied liquidity and signaled support for the financial system.</p><small>Rate connection: a dramatic market shock, but not the same kind of housing-credit collapse that would occur in 2008.</small></div></li>
          <li><span class="timeline-date">2000–2006</span><div><h4>The housing boom and easy-credit era</h4><p>Home prices climbed rapidly in many markets. Mortgage securitization expanded, and underwriting increasingly included riskier products, including subprime and low-documentation loans.</p><small>Housing connection: rising prices masked weak loan quality and encouraged leverage across households and financial institutions.</small></div></li>
          <li><span class="timeline-date">2007–2009</span><div><h4>The housing crash and Global Financial Crisis</h4><p>Mortgage delinquencies and foreclosures surged, mortgage-backed securities lost value, major financial firms failed or were rescued, and the economy entered the Great Recession.</p><small>Rate connection: the Fed cut short-term rates toward zero and later bought mortgage-backed securities; yet credit tightened and many borrowers could not qualify.</small></div></li>
          <li><span class="timeline-date">2010–2019</span><div><h4>Slow recovery and a long period of low rates</h4><p>Housing gradually recovered while the Fed kept policy rates unusually low for much of the decade, with gradual increases later in the expansion.</p><small>Housing connection: lower mortgage rates supported affordability and refinancing, although prices and recovery varied widely by region.</small></div></li>
          <li><span class="timeline-date">2020–2021</span><div><h4>COVID-19, emergency stimulus, and record-low mortgages</h4><p>The pandemic triggered an abrupt economic shutdown. The Fed cut rates to near zero and bought large amounts of Treasury and mortgage-backed securities; strong housing demand and limited supply pushed prices higher.</p><small>Rate connection: the 30-year mortgage annual average reached about 2.96% in 2021—an exceptional period, not a normal long-run benchmark.</small></div></li>
          <li><span class="timeline-date">2022–2025</span><div><h4>Inflation returns—and mortgage payments reset higher</h4><p>Supply bottlenecks, strong demand, energy pressures, and other forces drove inflation to multi-decade highs. The Fed raised its policy rate aggressively; mortgage rates rose sharply even as home prices remained elevated in many markets.</p><small>Housing connection: affordability was squeezed by the combination of higher prices and higher borrowing costs.</small></div></li>
        </ol>
        <p class="fine-print">Historical context, not a claim that one event alone determined mortgage rates. Mortgage pricing also reflects Treasury yields, mortgage-backed securities spreads, lender capacity, borrower risk, and loan characteristics.</p>
