        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="history-chart-card">
        <div class="history-chart-heading"><div><span class="history-kicker">EXPLORE THE DATA</span><h3 id="history-chart-title">U.S. home-price index</h3><p id="history-chart-subtitle">FHFA All-Transactions HPI · fourth-quarter observations · 1975–2025</p></div>
          <div class="history-chart-switch" role="group" aria-label="Choose historical chart"><button type="button" class="history-tab active" data-chart="prices" aria-pressed="true">Home values</button><button type="button" class="history-tab" data-chart="rates" aria-pressed="false">Mortgage rates</button></div>
        </div>
        <div class="history-chart-wrap"><svg id="housing-history-chart" viewBox="0 0 900 360" role="img" aria-label="Interactive historical chart of U.S. home values"></svg></div>
        <div class="history-chart-legend"><span><i class="legend-line"></i><span id="history-legend-label">FHFA home-price index (1980 Q1 = 100)</span></span><span id="history-chart-range">1975–2025</span></div>
        <p class="history-chart-note" id="history-chart-note">An index tracks changes over time; it is not the average dollar price of a home. The FHFA series uses repeat-sales and appraisal data for single-family properties associated with conventional conforming mortgages.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="history-payment-example">
        <div><span class="history-kicker">WHAT A RATE MEANS IN DOLLARS</span><h3>Compare the payment—not just the percentage.</h3><p>Illustrative monthly principal-and-interest payment on a $100,000, 30-year fixed loan. Taxes, insurance, mortgage insurance, HOA dues, points, and fees are excluded.</p></div>
        <div class="payment-rate-grid">
          <article><b>2.96%</b><strong>$419</strong><small>2021 annual average</small></article>
          <article><b>6.60%</b><strong>$639</strong><small>2025 annual average</small></article>
          <article><b>8.05%</b><strong>$737</strong><small>2000 annual average</small></article>
          <article><b>16.64%</b><strong>$1,396</strong><small>1981 annual average</small></article>
        </div>
        <p class="fine-print">A rate comparison is not the whole affordability story. Home prices, down payments, wages, property taxes, insurance, credit standards, and available loan products all affect the real monthly burden. The 2021 rate was historically exceptional; it is not a fair assumption for every future buyer.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="history-explainer-grid">
        <article><h3>How mortgage financing evolved</h3><ul>
          <li><b>1932:</b> Federal Home Loan Bank System created to support mortgage lending institutions.</li>
          <li><b>1934:</b> FHA created to insure qualifying mortgages and support broader access to home financing.</li>
          <li><b>1938:</b> Fannie Mae established to purchase FHA-insured mortgages and provide liquidity to lenders.</li>
          <li><b>1970:</b> Freddie Mac established; the secondary mortgage market continued to develop.</li>
          <li><b>1971:</b> Freddie Mac's long-running Primary Mortgage Market Survey begins, giving us a consistent national rate benchmark.</li>
          <li><b>1980s:</b> Mortgage rates reached extraordinary highs as the U.S. fought inflation.</li>
          <li><b>2007–2011:</b> The housing bust brought a major national home-price correction and tighter credit.</li>
          <li><b>2020–2021:</b> Mortgage rates reached record-low territory, followed by a sharp rate reset beginning in 2022.</li>
        </ul></article>
        <article><h3>Why the numbers need context</h3><ul>
          <li><b>Nominal vs. inflation-adjusted:</b> A home can rise in dollar value while losing purchasing power after inflation.</li>
          <li><b>National vs. local:</b> National averages can hide significant declines in individual cities, neighborhoods, and property types.</li>
          <li><b>Index vs. median price:</b> Repeat-sales indexes track price changes over time; median sale prices can shift when the mix of homes sold changes.</li>
          <li><b>Home value vs. investment return:</b> Appreciation does not include financing, taxes, insurance, repairs, vacancy, selling costs, or rental income.</li>
          <li><b>Mortgage rate vs. total cost:</b> Borrower rate depends on credit, points, loan type, down payment, property, and market conditions.</li>
        </ul></article>
      </div>
      <div class="history-callout"><b>National home-price declines have been relatively uncommon—but they do happen.</b><p>In the Shiller-based national annual series, home prices declined in 7 of 84 calendar years from 1942 through 2025: 1990, 1991, and 2007 through 2011. That is a small share of the years in this historical period, not a guarantee against future declines. National averages can hide sharper or more frequent declines in particular local markets, and inflation-adjusted results can differ.</p></div>
