    <details class="glossary-expand">
      <summary>
        <span class="section-label">INVESTOR &amp; MORTGAGE GLOSSARY</span>
        <strong>Mortgage terms, without the alphabet soup.</strong>
        <span class="glossary-toggle-hint">Tap to expand the glossary</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="glossary-content">
        <p class="section-intro">A practical reference to the terms investors may hear from mortgage brokers, lenders, appraisers, title companies, and property professionals. Definitions are general; program documents and lender rules control a specific transaction.</p>
<div class="resource-links">
        <a href="#glossary-property-value">Property value &amp; renovation</a>
        <a href="#glossary-rent-income">Rent &amp; income verification</a>
        <a href="#glossary-loan-structure">Loan structure &amp; qualification</a>
        <a href="#glossary-closing">Closing &amp; documentation</a>
      </div>

      <h3 class="standards-subhead" id="glossary-property-value">Property value &amp; renovation</h3>
      <div class="mortgage-standards-grid">
        <article><b>ARV — After-Repair Value</b><p>The estimated market value of a property after planned repairs or renovations are completed. Often used in fix-and-flip and rehab lending. ARV is an estimate, not a guaranteed sale price or appraisal result.</p></article>
        <article><b>As-Is Value</b><p>The property's estimated market value in its current condition, before proposed repairs or improvements.</p></article>
        <article><b>LTV — Loan-to-Value</b><p>The loan amount divided by the property's applicable value, expressed as a percentage. Example: a $200,000 loan against a $250,000 value is 80% LTV. The value used depends on the loan program.</p></article>
        <article><b>LTC — Loan-to-Cost</b><p>The loan amount divided by an eligible project cost basis, often purchase price plus approved renovation costs. Lenders define which costs count.</p></article>
        <article><b>Rehab / Renovation Budget</b><p>The estimated cost of repairs or improvements. Lenders may require a scope of work, contractor bids, inspections, draw controls, and contingency reserves.</p></article>
        <article><b>Comps — Comparable Sales</b><p>Recent sales of similar properties used by an appraiser or real estate professional to help estimate market value. Similarity, location, condition, and sale date matter.</p></article>
      </div>

      <h3 class="standards-subhead" id="glossary-rent-income">Rent &amp; income verification</h3>
      <div class="mortgage-standards-grid">
        <article><b>VOR — Verification of Rent</b><p>A record or third-party verification of a borrower's rental payment history, commonly used in mortgage underwriting to document whether rent was paid as agreed.</p></article>
        <article><b>VOM — Verification of Mortgage</b><p>A record or creditor verification of a borrower's existing mortgage payment history, including payment status and history where required.</p></article>
        <article><b>VOE — Verification of Employment</b><p>Confirmation of employment details, such as employer, position, and income, when required by the loan program.</p></article>
        <article><b>VOI / VOA — Verification of Income / Assets</b><p>Documentation or third-party confirmation of income or funds available to the borrower. Exact methods vary by program.</p></article>
        <article><b>NOI — Net Operating Income</b><p>Property operating income after ordinary operating expenses, before debt service and typically before income taxes, depreciation, and capital expenditures. Definitions can vary by lender or investor.</p></article>
        <article><b>DSCR — Debt-Service Coverage Ratio</b><p>A measure comparing qualifying property income with the property's debt obligations. In many residential DSCR programs, monthly qualifying rent is divided by monthly PITIA; lender formulas differ.</p></article>
        <article><b>Cap Rate — Capitalization Rate</b><p>Net operating income divided by property value or purchase price. It is a property-level yield measure before financing, not the borrower's cash-on-cash return.</p></article>
        <article><b>Cash-on-Cash Return</b><p>Annual pre-tax cash flow divided by the investor's cash invested. The result depends on how cash flow and invested cash are defined.</p></article>
      </div>

      <h3 class="standards-subhead" id="glossary-loan-structure">Loan structure &amp; qualification</h3>
      <div class="mortgage-standards-grid">
        <article><b>PITIA</b><p>Principal, interest, taxes, insurance, and association dues or assessments. A common housing-expense figure used in DSCR and other underwriting calculations.</p></article>
        <article><b>PITI</b><p>Principal, interest, taxes, and insurance. Unlike PITIA, this shorthand does not expressly include association dues.</p></article>
        <article><b>DTI — Debt-to-Income Ratio</b><p>Monthly qualifying debt obligations divided by qualifying gross monthly income. Commonly used in consumer mortgage underwriting; not the same as DSCR.</p></article>
        <article><b>CLTV — Combined Loan-to-Value</b><p>The total of the first mortgage and relevant subordinate liens divided by the applicable property value.</p></article>
        <article><b>HCLTV — Home Equity Line Combined LTV</b><p>A combined leverage calculation that generally considers the full available credit line of a HELOC, not just the amount currently drawn, under applicable agency rules.</p></article>
        <article><b>QM — Qualified Mortgage</b><p>A defined category of covered consumer mortgage that meets applicable federal requirements and can receive specified legal protections under the Ability-to-Repay rule.</p></article>
        <article><b>Non-QM — Non-Qualified Mortgage</b><p>A covered consumer mortgage that does not meet a QM definition. Non-QM is not synonymous with no-documentation, subprime, or automatically unsafe lending.</p></article>
        <article><b>ATR — Ability-to-Repay</b><p>For covered consumer mortgages, the creditor's required reasonable, good-faith determination that the borrower can repay according to the loan terms, subject to applicable rules and exemptions.</p></article>
        <article><b>IO — Interest-Only</b><p>A payment structure where scheduled payments cover interest only for a specified period; principal generally does not amortize during that period and payments may rise later.</p></article>
        <article><b>Prepayment Penalty</b><p>A fee that may apply when a borrower pays off a loan early, if permitted by applicable law and the loan contract. DSCR and business-purpose programs vary; review the note and rider.</p></article>
        <article><b>Seasoning</b><p>A required period of ownership, title history, loan history, or time since a prior event. The exact meaning and duration depend on the program.</p></article>
        <article><b>Reserves</b><p>Eligible funds a borrower must have available after closing, often measured in months of housing payments. Reserve definitions and acceptable assets vary by lender.</p></article>
      </div>

      <h3 class="standards-subhead" id="glossary-closing">Closing &amp; documentation</h3>
      <div class="mortgage-standards-grid">
        <article><b>EMD — Earnest Money Deposit</b><p>Funds a buyer deposits under a purchase contract to demonstrate good-faith intent. Contract terms govern how the deposit is credited, returned, or forfeited.</p></article>
        <article><b>POF — Proof of Funds</b><p>Documentation showing that a buyer or borrower has access to funds, often for down payment, closing costs, reserves, or a cash purchase.</p></article>
        <article><b>LOE — Letter of Explanation</b><p>A written explanation requested by an underwriter to clarify an item in the application or supporting documents. It should be accurate and supported where possible.</p></article>
        <article><b>CD — Closing Disclosure</b><p>A standardized disclosure of final loan terms and closing costs for covered consumer mortgage transactions, delivered within applicable timing requirements.</p></article>
        <article><b>LE — Loan Estimate</b><p>A standardized early disclosure of estimated terms and costs for covered consumer mortgage applications.</p></article>
        <article><b>LLC — Limited Liability Company</b><p>A legal business entity that may be used to hold investment property. Entity ownership, borrower eligibility, title, guarantees, and tax/legal consequences require transaction-specific review.</p></article>
        <article><b>MBS — Mortgage-Backed Security</b><p>A security backed by a pool of mortgage loans. Mortgage payments flow through the securitization structure to investors, subject to the security's terms and guarantees.</p></article>
        <article><b>GSE — Government-Sponsored Enterprise</b><p>In housing finance, commonly refers to Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac. They are shareholder-owned companies operating under federal charters and FHFA oversight, not ordinary federal agencies.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="fine-print">Abbreviations can have different meanings in different industries or lender systems. VOR and VOM are verification documents—not a guarantee of approval. ARV is not the same as an as-is appraisal. Always confirm the lender's written definition, calculation, and documentation requirements.</p>
    
      </div>
    </details>

